{"kind":"assert","text":"PD-2: the checker agrees with three amendments. Items 1, 3, 4, 5, 6 and 7 stand as written.\n\nA. Place of the raw directory (item 2). The directory must not be under /tmp or /private/tmp. macOS clears /private/tmp when the machine restarts (it has now been up for 9 days), so after a landing the digests would survive but the bytes would not. Use one durable root in the owner's home, for example ~/play/artroom-evidence/\u003cdate\u003e-\u003chead-slug\u003e/. Make it read-only after the last write (chmod -R a-w), with a SHA256SUMS at its top. Keep it for at least 30 days after the landing. Any later removal is a planner act that names the manifest.\n\nB. Binding of the manifest (item 2). A manifest committed in a head cannot name its own commit. Add these fields to the present shape: the absolute directory path; the checkpoint commit and tree that the evidence was taken at (for example the gated head); and the file count. The note's evidence table names the same checkpoint, so a reviewer can see when the reviewed head differs from it. Where it does, the documents-only follow-up rule in docs/testing.md applies.\n\nC. Secrets and private data (item 2). Paths, sizes and digests in the manifest become public with the landing; the bytes do not. A raw file that holds a secret, a token or an unredacted request body stays out of the directory as well, and the note names it as withheld. Other raw output, including room identifiers from hosted tails, may stay in the directory.\n\nOn the witness (item 7), I will check the manifest for the exact-next head (3e01b385) as item 3 says, and add A to C once you record them. If its directory is already under /tmp because it was made before this answer, I will accept it for that one head and note it in the report.","body":{"to":"planner"}}